          "label": "C",
          "text": "1",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "-1",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "solution": "Shortcut: Use King's property ∫_0^a f(x) dx = ∫_0^a f(a-x) dx. Let I = ∫_0^(π/2) log(tan x) dx. Replacing x by π/2 - x gives I = ∫_0^(π/2) log(cot x) dx = ∫_0^(π/2) -log(tan x) dx = -I. Hence 2I = 0 ⇒ I = 0. Step-by-step: I = ∫_0^(π/2) log(sin x) dx - ∫_0^(π/2) log(cos x) dx. Since ∫_0^(π/2) log(sin x) dx = ∫_0^(π/2) log(cos x) dx (by symmetry), the difference is zero.",
